bluez: install the Sixaxis HID record before asking the agent

A PS3 clone here re-enumerates as an X-input pad (045E:028E) about 120 ms
after it appears on USB. The sixaxis plugin only installs the HID record from
agent_auth_cb(), which runs when the D-Bus authorization agent replies, and
that round trip does not fit in the window.

Observed on hardware, with the kernel-side race already fixed by
005-hid-core-hidraw-before-hidinput.patch:

  497  sixaxis: setting up new device      <- first cable pass, plugin won
       ... six incoming connections, every one refused:
       input_device_set_channel() idev (nil) psm 17
       Refusing input device connect: No such file or directory (2)
       confirm_event_cb() Refusing connection: unknown device
  688  sixaxis: setting up new device      <- second cable pass
  700  agent_auth_cb() remote ... old_central == new_central

agent_auth_cb() appears once, after the second pass. There is no "Agent
replied negatively" and the device is never removed: the pad simply goes away
before the agent answers, so btd_device_set_record() is never reached. The
device stays trusted but without UUID 00001124, input_device_register() never
runs, and the pad cannot connect until a second cable pairing completes.

Install the record in setup_device() instead. It is the canonical Sixaxis
record already defined in this file, so nothing is queried over the air, and
the HID profile exists from the first millisecond regardless of the agent.
The central address is still written from agent_auth_cb(), which now wins its
race thanks to the kernel patch.

Also narrow the "already known, skipping" guard to connected devices. With
001-trust-sixaxis.patch every pad is marked trusted as soon as it is seen, so
once the record is installed early that guard would fire on the second cable
pass and skip a pad whose central address had never been written - exactly the
pad coming from another console. Upstream's intent for that check is a pad
plugged in to charge while already in use, which is_connected() covers.

diff --git a/plugins/sixaxis.c b/plugins/sixaxis.c
--- a/plugins/sixaxis.c
+++ b/plugins/sixaxis.c
@@ -342,8 +342,13 @@ static bool setup_device(int fd, const char *sysfs_path,
 	device = btd_adapter_find_device(adapter, &device_bdaddr,
 							BDADDR_BREDR);
-	if (device && btd_device_has_uuid(device, HID_UUID) &&
-			(btd_device_is_connected(device) ||
-			 btd_device_is_trusted(device))) {
+	/*
+	 * Only skip a pad that is really in use. The batocera patch marks
+	 * every pad trusted the moment it is seen, so keeping is_trusted()
+	 * here would also skip a pad whose cable pairing never finished,
+	 * and its central address would never be written.
+	 */
+	if (device && btd_device_has_uuid(device, HID_UUID) &&
+			btd_device_is_connected(device)) {
 		char device_addr[18];
 		ba2str(&device_bdaddr, device_addr);
 		DBG("device %s already known, skipping", device_addr);
@@ -364,6 +369,22 @@ static bool setup_device(int fd, const char *sysfs_path,
 	btd_device_set_temporary(device, false);
 	btd_device_set_trusted(device, true);
 
+	/*
+	 * Install the Sixaxis HID record here, not only in agent_auth_cb():
+	 * some clones re-enumerate as an X-input pad about 120 ms after they
+	 * appear, and the D-Bus round trip to the authorization agent does
+	 * not always fit in that window. When it does not, the device is left
+	 * trusted but without the HID UUID, input_device_register() never
+	 * runs, and every incoming connection from the pad is refused with
+	 * "unknown device" until a second cable pairing happens.
+	 *
+	 * The record is the canonical Sixaxis one defined in this file, so
+	 * this costs no SDP query over the air.
+	 */
+	if (cp->type == CABLE_PAIRING_SIXAXIS)
+		btd_device_set_record(device, HID_UUID,
+					SIXAXIS_HID_SDP_RECORD);
+
 	closure = g_new0(struct authentication_closure, 1);
 	if (!closure) {
 		btd_adapter_remove_device(adapter, device);
